course: dictionary
lesson: 11977
title: Formal words: censure
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Censure is a long, serious word, and you can work out its meaning from the clues around it. «The council voted to censure the councillor for telling lies.» Telling lies is wrong, and a council voting on it is official, so censure must mean an official telling-off.
explain: As a verb, to censure someone means to criticise them strongly and officially. «The judge censured the man for breaking the rules.» Swap your meaning into the sentence: «The judge criticised the man strongly and officially for breaking the rules.» It still makes sense, so your meaning is right.
explain: A vote of censure is a group's official way of showing it disapproves of a leader or member. «The parliament held a vote of censure against the minister.» Here censure is a formal punishment, much stronger than a friendly reminder to behave.
fact: Censure means strong, official criticism or disapproval.
fact: To censure someone is to criticise them strongly and officially, especially for something they did wrong.
fact: A vote of censure is when a parliament or group officially says it disapproves of a leader or member.
fact: In politics and law, censure is a formal punishment, for example when the US Congress censures a member who has broken the rules.
fact: Censure is a formal word for telling someone off; simpler words for it are criticise or reprimand.
exercise: «The council voted to censure the councillor for lying.» What does censure mean here? | * Officially criticise and disapprove | Forget about it | Cheer loudly | Give a prize
exercise: What is a vote of censure? | A vote to give a leader a medal | A vote about what to eat for lunch | * An official vote showing a group disapproves of a leader or member | A quiet whisper in a meeting
exercise: Which simple word is closest in meaning to the verb censure? | * Criticise | Hide | Praise | Forget
test: «The newspaper faced public censure after printing false stories.» What does public censure mean? | Being ignored by the public | Rewards from everyone | A quiet walk in the park | * Strong, open criticism from the public
test: Which word is a formal way to say to tell someone off strongly and officially? | Hum | * Censure | Doze | Praise
exam: In «The parliament passed a motion to censure the minister», what does censure mean? | Forgave the minister completely | * Made a formal statement of strong disapproval | Elected the minister as leader | Thanked the minister warmly
